course: dictionary
lesson: 2243
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Cradle can be a thing, a place, a frame or an action. «Rock-a-bye baby, down will come baby, cradle and all» is a lullaby, so here cradle means a small bed for a baby.
explain: «In Newton's cradle, swing one ball in and one flies out the other side.» The word cradle is a frame that holds the balls. Once you know the clues, you can pick the right meaning. Ask yourself: is it a thing, a place or an action?
explain: Cradle can also be a verb. «She cradled the baby gently in her arms» tells you that cradle means to hold something carefully. «The cradle of civilisation» uses cradle for a place where something began.
fact: A cradle is a small bed for a baby, often one that can rock.
fact: A cradle can also be a frame that holds something, like a phone cradle or the swinging balls in Newton's cradle.
fact: To cradle something means to hold it gently in your arms.
fact: The cradle of civilisation means the place where something began, such as the first great human cultures.
fact: «From the cradle to the grave» means from the very start of life to the very end.
exercise: The baby slept in her cradle while the lullaby played. What is a cradle here? | A kind of boat for the sea | A long road up a hill | *A small bed for a baby, often one that can rock
exercise: Historians call that region the cradle of civilisation. What does cradle mean here? | A small bed for a baby | A frame that holds a phone | A soft blanket | *The place where something began
exercise: She cradled the kitten gently in her arms. What does cradled mean? | Threw something high in the air | Counted things quietly | Washed something carefully | *Held something gently in your arms
test: «Skyscraper windows are cleaned from a cradle that hangs from rails on the roof.» What is a cradle in this sentence? | To hold something gently | *A frame or platform that holds workers and hangs from rails | A small bed for a baby | The place where something began
test: Ella says, «The cradle rocked as the baby fell asleep.» Which meaning fits best? | To hold something gently in your arms | A frame that holds a phone | The place where something began | *A small bed for a baby, often one that can rock
exam: Which sentence uses cradle as a verb, meaning to hold something gently? | *Dad cradled the sleeping baby in his arms | The cradle of civilisation is an old and important idea | Newton's cradle has five shiny metal balls | The cradle hung from the roof of the tall building
